n, target selection, monorepo build_root/source_dir rules, and backward compatibility; (3) exact access behavior: syntax-only offline warning, install/audit hard failure when inaccessible, credential ownership, allowed Git transports, local operator substitutions, moved refs, submodules, LFS, symlinks and special files; (4) snapshot and audit equivalence to in-skill source, including compiler-visible root, dependency graph containment, source identity, receipts, marker v2 and protected cache; (5) deterministic build, install, PATH/shim verification and rollback; (6) platform-owned signing policy and the boundary between local signing and release-time notarization; (7) the extension rule for future Rust, Swift, Kotlin/JVM, C/C++, .NET and other closed drivers; (8) exact impact on existing spec, Curator, cocoaskills and interoperability tasks, while Linux remains a separate later native validation. Explicitly reconcile the user proposal that the repository descriptor name the final binary and output location with the accepted protocol rule forbidding package-controlled executable names and output selection. Prefer logical target IDs plus manager-derived command name and private staging path unless evidence supports another closed design. Audit must happen before any compiler execution. Repository access failure must warn during offline/static check when verification cannot be attempted and must fail install before mutation when source cannot be resolved. Credentials and signing identities must never come from the package descriptor. Research authoritative Git, Go build, platform signing/notarization and sandbox constraints as needed. Persist the decision and threat model as a new task-scoped outcome resource, include concrete JSON examples and normative MUST/MUST NOT recommendations, record source links, then route to to-review for an independent architecture review.\\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: codex via explicit_override\\nspawn launch composition: deg